{"qid":"Q33946","names":["Czechoslovakia"],"title":"Czechoslovakia","endonym":{"native":"Československo","lang":"cs"},"capsule":"Czechoslovakia was proclaimed on 28 October 1918 as a successor state to Austria-Hungary, uniting Czechs, Slovaks, and the Subcarpathian Ruthenians under president Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk; it was among the most industrialized and democratically stable states in interwar Central Europe. The Munich Agreement of September 1938 ceded the Sudetenland to Nazi Germany, followed by complete German occupation in March 1939 and the imposition of a Slovak puppet state. Restored after 1945, the country underwent a communist coup in February 1948 and Soviet-led Warsaw Pact intervention crushed Alexander Dubček's Prague Spring reforms in August 1968. The Velvet Revolution of November 1989 peacefully ended communist rule under Václav Havel; on 31 December 1992 the country dissolved by parliamentary agreement into the Czech Republic and Slovakia.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Czechoslovakia's boundaries are precisely documented by interwar treaties and post-1945 adjustments (notably the transfer of Subcarpathian Ruthenia to the USSR in 1945); territorial extent is among the best attested in this dataset.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Czechoslovakia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Czechoslovakia"},{"label":"Britannica: Czechoslovakia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Czechoslovakia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Velvet Revolution","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velvet_Revolution"}],"britannica":"place/Czechoslovakia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Czechoslovakia","ru